 prose-scrub is the gate I built with Claude yesterday. It fails prose on dashes, stock phrases, pre-negation and flat rhythm. Today we added a second reader: a command called judge that asks Claude Haiku 5.5 about two habits no word list can see, five times per file. Then I ran the whole thing on four of my own posts and my résumé, read the results in a side-by-side viewer, and found the place where the gate makes my writing worse. I'm shipping it anyway, as 0.4.0. This is the build log.
 
+*Figure: A page of ten text lines passes two readers. A sweep cuts three lines the word-list gate can name, shown in red and shrunk. Then five dots appear beside two other lines, which turn green: the judge's five runs flag them and leave the decision to a person.*
+
 ## A bet about headings
 
 Wikipedia's guide to signs of AI writing lists undue emphasis on significance: text that keeps telling you its subject matters. I suspected my last post did it, so I asked for a test: "Run a haiku across the prose in the md and ask it to rank the headings from 0.0 to 1.0 on "how important does the author want me to think this section is" and I bet it'll rank every heading as 1.0".
 
 I lost. Three Haiku runs gave means of 0.47, 0.52 and 0.50, from 0.05 for the source list up to 0.90 for the section on the first relaxation. But all three runs quoted the same seven lines as the reason a section sounded important, lines like "The one that changed the plan was" and "One line in that summary gave it away". Those seven were the real finding. The rewrite cut them.
 
+*Figure: Three bars for three Haiku runs show mean heading importance of 0.47, 0.52 and 0.50, each well short of a line at 1.0 marking the bet that every heading would rank 1.0. Below, seven short green bars stand for the seven lines all three runs quoted.*
+
 Each of those runs was a Claude Code subagent, and each cost about 65,000 tokens. I asked for a cheaper version that sends only the headings, and Claude added a headings command. It saved almost nothing: 58,500 tokens instead of 65,000, because nearly all of a subagent's cost is its own setup, not the post. Calling the API directly with a two-line system prompt costs a few thousand tokens for a whole post. So the judge talks to the API, with a key kept in the macOS Keychain where neither the command line nor Claude ever sees it.
 
